  about: [
    `<em>Romeo and Juliet</em> is the play that defined what Western culture means by young love. A boy from the Montague house crashes a Capulet party on a Sunday evening, sees a girl across the room, and forgets the woman he was sighing over an hour earlier. They speak fourteen lines together — a sonnet, exactly — and kiss before they know each other's names. By morning they have sworn marriage; by afternoon a friar has secretly married them; by evening Juliet's cousin has killed Romeo's friend, and Romeo has killed the cousin. By Friday morning both lovers are dead in the family vault.`,
    `Shakespeare wrote it in the mid-1590s, early in his career, drawing on Arthur Brooke's narrative poem and compressing Brooke's nine-month timeline into five days. The verse is lyrical in a way the later tragedies are not — Mercutio's Queen Mab speech, Juliet's "gallop apace, you fiery-footed steeds," the shared sonnet at first meeting, the balcony scene — and many of the most-quoted lines in English come from this play. It has been a stage favorite for four hundred years partly because it understands that this is what young love feels like from the inside: total, immediate, unanswerable.`,
  ],
  chaptersSubtitle: 'All 25 scenes, by act and beat — from the Chorus\'s sonnet to the bodies in the vault.',
  chaptersLead: `<p>Romeo and Juliet is built across a Prologue and five acts of unequal length, twenty-five scenes in all. The Chorus opens the play by telling the audience exactly how it ends. Act I is the brawl, the party, the meeting. Act II is the courtship — balcony, friar, marriage — all inside a single Monday morning. Act III is the catastrophe: Mercutio and Tybalt dead by noon, Romeo banished by sundown, Juliet ordered to marry Paris by Thursday. Act IV is the desperate plan and the false death. Act V is the tomb.</p>`,
  themesByline: 'Five threads through the play',
  themesLead: `Romeo and Juliet is a love story that wants to be more than a love story. Shakespeare took a nine-month source poem and compressed it into five days, and the speed is not a flaw but the argument. The play asks how much a teenager's feeling weighs against an inherited feud, a parent's pride, a friar's good intentions, and an undelivered letter. Its answer is not the one a love story usually gives.`,

